	1. Exploring Authentication Concepts
		Authentication is very important step, as without authentication everyone is anonymous then everyone has the same access to all resources. It's not just limited to users. Services, processes, workstations, network devices all use authentication to prove their identities. Many computers use mutual authentication where both parties authenticate each other.
		a. Comparing Identification and AAA
			§ Authentication, authorization, and accounting work together with identification to provide a comprehensive access management system.
			§ Identity = user claim of who they are by username or email id
			§ Authentication = with password user proves identity is real
			§ Authorization = users are granted access to resources based on their proven identity
			§ Accounting = these methods track user activity and record it in logs to create audit trail
		b. Comparing Authentication Factors
			§ Often simplified as types or factors of authentication. Authentication is often simplified as types, or factors, of authentication. A use case of supporting authentication may require administrators to implement one factor of authentication for basic authentication, two factors for more secure authentication, or more factors for higher security. As an introduction, the factors are:
			i. Something You Know / Type I
				This authentication factor refers to a shared secret, such as password or even a pin. It's the least secure form of authentication but complexity can be added up.
				□ Password complexity
					® A strong password is of sufficient length, doesn't include words found in dictionary or any part of user's name and combines upper case, lower case, numbers and special characters.
					® A small complex password is also not strong, it should be long enough as well.
					® Too complex passwords are also less secure passwords as people tend to write them down somewhere
					® Instead of passwords users are encouraged to use passphrases.
				□ Training users about password behaviors
					Organize should train their employees about secure passwords if they use passwords in organization. This includes both creation of strong passwords and the importance of never giving out their passwords and not using common guessable passwords
				□ Password expiration
					Use should change their passwords regularly in 45  or 90 days. Some systems force users to change passwords technically(no login until they change passwords)
				□ Password recovery
					® Before password reset in case of someone forgot their password, user's identity has to be verified (else it will just give hacker access to user's account).
					® In some cases, help desk manually changes user's password and let user know, but this causes another problem as 2 people know the password now. Instead of this, help desk should generate a temporary password which user can change after he logs in.
					® Some systems asks you different questions for identity proofing and then only lets user reset the password and some system sends PIN to user's mobile phone, if only correct code is entered then only user can change the password.
				□ Password history and password reuse
					Password history system remembers past passwords and prevents users from reusing passwords.
				□ Group policy
					® Windows group policy  allows administrator to configure a setting once in a GPO and apply this setting to many users and computers in a domain.
					® ADDS = Active Directory Domain Services, is directory service microsoft developed for windows domain networks.
					® The policy only needs to be set once to apply all systems in the domain.
					® Group Policy is implemented on a domain controller within a domain. Administrators use it to create password policies, implement security settings, configure host-based firewalls, and much more.
				□ Using a password policy
					® Enforce password history
						To avoid password reuse
					® Maximum password age
						Shouldn't use same password for long period
					® Minimum password age
						Frequent password changes may lead back to reuse original password
					® Minimum password length
						To add complexity
					® Password must meet complexity requirements
						Combine different kinds of characters
					® Store passwords using reversible encryption
						It saves passwords in a way that original password can be discovered. It is rarely enabled.
				□ Implementing account lockout policies
					® Prevents users from guessing the password.
					® Account lockout threshold = maximum number of times a user can enter the wrong password
					® Account lockout duration = indicates how long an account remains locked.
				□ Changing default passwords
					® You should change default passwords(like 'admin') before putting it to use else anyone can log into the system can gain access.
					® It also includes changing default account name if possible.
					® Some administrator add a dummy user account named 'administrator' which has no permissions. If someone tried to guess the password for this account, it will lock user out and alert the administrators about the illicit activity.
			ii. Something You Have / Type II
				Refers to something you can physically hold.
				1) Smart cards
					® Stores details from certificate which provides certificate based authentication. Embedded certificate allows use of complex encryption key and provides much more secure authentication
					® Provides confidentiality, integrity, authentication and non-repudiation.
					® Smart requires Embedded certificate [holds user's private key and matches with public key] and Public Key Infrastructure(PKI)
					® Often used as another factor of authentication. e.g. also be needed to enter password or PIN.
				2) CACs and PIVs
					® CAC = Common Access Card  is specialized smart card used by U.S.Dept. of Defense.  Is also includes picture of user required for as a part of photo identification to gain access to secure facility.
					® PIV = Personal Identity Verification. Special type of smart card used by U.S. federal agencies. It also includes photo identification and provides confidentiality, integrity, authentication, and non- repudiation for the users, just as a CAC does.
					® CAC and PIV both support dual-factor authentication.
				3) Tokens or Key Fobs
					® Token/fob is an electronics device about the size of car key with small lcd displaying a numbers which keeps getting refreshed after 60 seconds.
					® The token is synced with server that knows what the number is at each moment.
					® This number Is one time use rolling passwords and is not useful for attackers for very long time as it gets refreshed within 60 seconds.
					® Used for dual factor authentication along with passwords  and authenticate via website.
				4) HOTP (HMAC based OTP) and TOTP (Time based OTP)
					® HMAC(hash based authentication code) based one-time password (HOTP) is an open standard used for creating onetime passwords.
					® The algorithm combines a secret key and an incrementing counter, and uses HMAC to create a hash of the result. It then converts result into an HOTP value of six to eight digits.
					® A password created with HOTP remains valid until it’s used. Suppose Bart requested the HOTP number but then got distracted and never used it. What happens now? Theoretically, it remains usable forever. This presents a risk related to HOTP because other people can use the password if they discover it.
					® A Time-based One-Time Password (TOTP) is similar to HOTP, but it uses a timestamp instead of a counter. One-time passwords created with TOTP typically expire after 30 seconds.
					® One significant benefit of HOTP and TOTP is price. Hardware tokens that use these open source standards are significantly less expensive than tokens that use proprietary algorithms.
			iii. Something You Are / Type III
				Uses biometric for authentication. Stronger as comparison to passwords.
				Aka third factor authentication
				Biometric Methods
					® Fingerprint scanner, retina scanner, iris scanner, voice recognition, facial recognition
					® Some manufacturer can take shortcuts in device manufacturing resulting into fallowing errors
					® False acceptance device incorrectly identifies unauthorized user as authorized one
					® False rejection device incorrectly rejects an authorized user.
					® After each error, you can adjust the sensitivity of system. By increasing sensitivity, it decreases number of false matches and increases number of false rejections.
					® In contrast, decreasing sensitivity  increases false matches and decreases false rejections.
					® 
					
					
					® CER (Crossover error rate), a low CER == more accurate system
			iv. Somewhere You Are
				□ Identifies user's location. Many authentication systems use the IP address for geolocation as IP address provides information in the country, region, state, city and sometimes zip
				□ IP address for geolocation isn't foolproof, as VPN can change IP
			v. Something You Do
				□ Refers to  actions you can take such as gestures on touch screen. e.g. windows 10 supports picture passwords, user has to select a picture and then they can add three gestures as their  picture password.
				□ Another example is keystroke dynamics measure the pattern and rhythm as a user types on a keyboard.
				□ Many professionals refer to this as behavioral biometrics.
			vi. Dual-Factor and Multifactor Authentication
				□ Uses 2 different factors of authentication such as something you have and something you know.
				□ Multifactor authentication uses more than 2 factors of authentication
				□ Using 2 methods of authentication in the same factor is NOT dual factor authentication.
		c. Summarizing Identification Methods
			Different identification methods are username, photo identification, and biometrics
			Most commonly used identification method is username. 
			CAC and PIVs include a picture and other information about the owner-something you have factor
			Something you are factor is based on biometrics for authentication.
